WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:26.020
Okay, so we're back at the screen. We're
now going to look at generating
international trade ideas in individual
equities, like we did in the previous
video using the ISM, but now we're going
to be looking at ADRs, American Depository
Receipts, which are essentially
international companies, so non-domestic
U.S. companies.

00:00:26.020 --> 00:00:51.680
So we're looking at companies outside the
United States with listings on U.S.
exchanges as ADRs. Now this video is going
to be relatively shorter than most videos
in the PTM video series, and that's
because this is really just an exercise in
organization, common sense, and
processing.

00:00:51.680 --> 00:01:04.100
Now, if you recall, the macro dashboard,
essentially what we're doing here is we're
looking at the right-hand side now of the
macroeconomic leading indicator dashboard.

00:01:04.100 --> 00:01:21.960
When it comes to American Depository
Receipts, so international companies with
U.S. dollar-denominated stock listings on
U.S. domestic exchanges, when it comes to
ADRs, there is a universe of ADRs.

00:01:23.020 --> 00:01:42.460
At any one time, there's going to be a
certain amount of ADRs that are currently
listed on U.S. exchanges, and of course,
those stocks, those companies, will have
exposure to different geographies around
the world.

00:01:43.240 --> 00:02:05.120
However, their ADR listings mean they
report in U.S. dollars, and similarly,
even U.S. companies that have
international exposure in their revenues
and earnings will have exposure to certain
international geographies outside the
United States.

00:02:05.120 --> 00:02:22.560
So whether a company is listed in the U.S.
as an international ADR, denominated in
U.S. dollars, or it's a U.S. company with
international exposure, regardless, if a
company has international exposure,

00:02:22.560 --> 00:02:34.660
we, of course, need to have resources to
enable us to appraise the international
exposure in establishing a portfolio bias.

00:02:35.420 --> 00:02:48.920
So what we'll be doing in this video is
looking at this in the context of ADRs, so
not U.S. domestic companies with
international exposure, but the same
disciplines would apply.

00:02:49.780 --> 00:03:08.900
So, for example, a German company has an
ADR listing, and they have large exposure
with their revenues and earnings in
Germany, maybe also in Italy, maybe also
across the rest of the European Union.

00:03:08.900 --> 00:03:32.220
So, of course, we need to be on top of
German, Italian, and European
macroeconomics in order to have a
portfolio bias, a bias on German stocks
that have exposure, or companies that have
exposure predominantly to the German
economy, plus the Italian economy, and
even the wider European economy.

00:03:33.000 --> 00:03:49.120
So if we are establishing a country bias,
and therefore potentially a stock bias, in
stocks that have exposure to certain
geographies, we need to be on top of a
number of things from a macro perspective.

00:03:49.800 --> 00:04:10.120
So we want to be on top of our money
market indicators in that particular
geography, we want to be on top of any
surveys, and we also want to be on top of
international cyclical commodity prices,
and whether we're seeing demand-led or
supply-led price increases or decreases.

00:04:11.120 --> 00:04:26.440
So, yes, we use our macroeconomic leading
indicator dashboard to get an overall
portfolio bias, but we do also use the
macro dashboard in getting a geographical
bias as well.

00:04:26.760 --> 00:04:28.560
It can also be used for that.

00:04:28.560 --> 00:04:47.600
And on top of that, it can also be used
for generating trade ideas in individual
equities, not only domestically in the
U.S., so for U.S.-listed domestic stocks,
but also for international ADRs.

00:04:48.160 --> 00:05:00.440
So what we're going to focus on here,
because we've already covered
international surveys in Europe and China,
we're going to focus here on the IHS
market PMIs.

00:05:00.680 --> 00:05:10.560
So on the macro dashboard, you'll see the
IHS market PMIs mentioned for Japan, U.K.,
Brazil, and Canada.

00:05:10.560 --> 00:05:16.940
And there is actually, in fact, a market
PMI index for most countries.

00:05:17.500 --> 00:05:27.520
Now, we also mentioned earlier in the
video series about these things called
American Depository Receipts, ADRs, when
we were actually discussing China.

00:05:28.340 --> 00:05:32.260
What ADRs actually are, are essentially
secondary listings.

00:05:32.260 --> 00:05:40.740
So it's companies that have their primary
listing in another country denominated in
the currency of that country.

00:05:41.840 --> 00:05:55.680
And what a lot of companies actually do is
also choose to list equity in the U.S.
domestic market as American depository
receipts denominated in U.S. dollars.

00:05:55.680 --> 00:06:07.040
And this is usually so institutional
investors in the U.S., so most
importantly, U.S. pension money, can
maintain custody holdings onshore.

00:06:07.040 --> 00:06:12.020
So it's lower risk, and it's in, well,
denominated in U.S. dollars.

00:06:12.560 --> 00:06:20.500
And it's a win-win for both sides, because
the pension funds, U.S. pension funds, get
to invest in these companies,

00:06:20.720 --> 00:06:24.660
and the companies get to raise capital in
the United States.

00:06:25.680 --> 00:06:33.180
Now, if you recall from earlier in the
video series, when we covered ADRs, when
looking at China, we covered them briefly
there.

00:06:34.220 --> 00:06:41.340
ADRs can trade on exchange, and they can
trade off exchange, so over-the-counter
OTC.

00:06:42.040 --> 00:06:49.960
What we're interested in, as traders and
portfolio managers, we're interested in
only the on-exchange ADRs,

00:06:49.960 --> 00:06:54.600
so the ones that we can access and trade
on our brokerage platforms.

00:06:55.600 --> 00:07:05.720
On top of that, many of these ADRs have
listed options also, but some don't, so we
always have to be aware of that.

00:07:06.440 --> 00:07:14.940
It's basically a function of market
capitalization or size of the company and
the liquidity of the stock.

00:07:15.940 --> 00:07:24.920
Now, up to this point, we've been looking
at a universe of around 2,200 stocks in
the U.S. domestically,

00:07:25.720 --> 00:07:33.020
and what we can do when we start looking
internationally is we can increase our
opportunity set.

00:07:33.060 --> 00:07:43.400
So we can use our international leading
indicators, and we can use the IHS market
PMIs and American depository receipts

00:07:43.400 --> 00:07:50.560
to increase the opportunity set from 2,200
stocks to 2,600 stocks.

00:07:51.240 --> 00:07:59.980
So at the time of recording, we're looking
at around 400 ADRs in terms of our ADR
opportunity set.

00:08:00.900 --> 00:08:03.100
And of course, this will change in the
future.

00:08:03.680 --> 00:08:05.040
There'll be more, there'll be less.

00:08:05.820 --> 00:08:06.700
It doesn't really matter.

00:08:07.100 --> 00:08:13.760
What you need to focus on here is the
actual process, and we're going to give
you all of the resources here to do that.

00:08:15.020 --> 00:08:26.260
So when it comes to the market PMIs, we
can use them in exactly the same way as we
use the ISM PMIs inside the U.S.

00:08:26.860 --> 00:08:34.100
However, the data for all of the countries
covered globally is very highly priced.

00:08:35.480 --> 00:08:41.980
So IHS market is a company, and they do
sell this data.

00:08:43.280 --> 00:08:53.180
Unfortunately, it's very, very highly
priced, costs a lot to purchase it up
front, and maintain all of the data on a
continual basis.

00:08:53.900 --> 00:09:00.540
You would literally have to pay IHS
market, the company, hundreds of thousands
of dollars to get it.

00:09:01.120 --> 00:09:09.020
And you'd be paying an annual subscription
per user for tens of thousands of dollars
on a continuous basis.

00:09:09.840 --> 00:09:17.840
The company itself, IHS market, is
actually a public company, and the company
is registered in Bermuda.

00:09:18.740 --> 00:09:21.160
But they do have a listing in the U.S.

00:09:21.940 --> 00:09:34.460
And the data product that they sell for
PMIs is essentially aimed at institutional
traders, at investment banks and hedge
funds, and also investors.

00:09:35.120 --> 00:09:44.400
So really, we're looking at long-only
asset managers and long-short portfolio
managers as their clients, not retail
traders.

00:09:45.400 --> 00:09:54.900
So the price point for retail traders to
get hold of all of this data and manage it
on a continuous basis is just not viable.

00:09:55.300 --> 00:10:04.640
However, non-institutional traders, like
retail traders, can still benefit from the
press releases.

00:10:05.400 --> 00:10:17.360
And you can use this information in the
same way as we did for the ISM in the
previous video to take positions in ADRs
that are denominated in USD.

00:10:18.200 --> 00:10:23.220
So we do have a download here that
accompanies this video.

00:10:23.780 --> 00:10:29.340
So go to the downloads area and download
the spreadsheet ADRs list.

00:10:29.340 --> 00:10:42.720
This is going to help you understand how
we actually process for individual ADR or
individual equities, trade ideas in ADRs,
utilizing macro data.

00:10:43.520 --> 00:10:47.460
So this is just common sense processing.

00:10:47.760 --> 00:10:59.760
But we've put this all together for you in
a template to create an opportunity set
for tradable ADRs at the time of making
the video.

00:11:00.400 --> 00:11:11.020
At any point in the future, you would just
update this spreadsheet and use it as a
template and utilize the resources
mentioned in the video.

00:11:11.020 --> 00:11:20.220
And of course, if resources change in the
future, then you yourself, you're going to
have to go and find other sources.

00:11:21.260 --> 00:11:23.080
Concentrate on the process, guys.

00:11:23.860 --> 00:11:26.420
It's the process that matters here.

00:11:27.240 --> 00:11:38.980
So in putting the sheet together here, we
basically revisit the source for ADR
information that we referred to earlier in
the PTM video series.

00:11:38.980 --> 00:11:43.500
So topforeignstocks.com, foreign ADRs
list.

00:11:44.580 --> 00:11:55.520
And there, what you're going to find is on
exchange listed, so tradable ADRs, and
they're split up into geographical regions
first, then by country.

00:11:56.480 --> 00:12:05.020
And within each country, you're going to
find ADRs that trade both as on exchange
and as OTC, over the counter.

00:12:05.020 --> 00:12:13.140
And what you do here is you get a bit
dirty, get a bit naughty, and you scrape
the data from the website.

00:12:13.500 --> 00:12:18.560
So all you do is select the data and cut
and paste it into a spreadsheet.

00:12:19.280 --> 00:12:26.580
And this is what we've done for the
spreadsheet in the downloads area ADRs
list and organized it all properly.

00:12:26.620 --> 00:12:38.860
So what we do here is we calculate the
opportunity set of foreign ADRs listed in
the U.S., and we organize the data
properly by sector.

00:12:39.580 --> 00:12:48.580
So with the template and the download, all
the initial work has been done for you,
organizing the data in that spreadsheet.

00:12:49.200 --> 00:12:59.360
However, on a continuous basis, you're
going to have to maintain the sheet
yourself, and generally speaking, you're
going to be updating it at least once,
possibly twice a year.

00:13:00.360 --> 00:13:07.920
ADR listings don't change very frequently,
so you'll be infrequently updating this
spreadsheet.

00:13:09.260 --> 00:13:12.100
So what do we find when we put this
spreadsheet together?

00:13:12.740 --> 00:13:18.860
Well, there's 415 foreign ADRs at the time
of recording that are tradable.

00:13:19.080 --> 00:13:25.820
So they're traded on exchange at the time
of recording this video, writing the
presentation on the screen.

00:13:25.820 --> 00:13:36.860
If we look at the data by country, we can
see that the foreign ADR market is very
much dominated by China, mainland Europe,
and the U.K.,

00:13:36.860 --> 00:13:42.360
making approximately combined 70% of all
ADRs in total.

00:13:43.440 --> 00:13:51.720
And then when we look at China's
contribution to listed tradable ADRs,
China makes up around 37% of all ADRs.

00:13:51.720 --> 00:13:57.000
So this is around half of the ADRs when we
add up China, Europe, and the U.K.

00:13:58.420 --> 00:14:02.820
Now, what's more important to us is the
sector data.

00:14:03.500 --> 00:14:10.800
We can't simply look at market data for a
particular country, then go into the ADR
market,

00:14:10.800 --> 00:14:22.380
and then just go long or short a
particular stock because the macro data in
the country where a stock has its primary
listing was either good or bad.

00:14:23.580 --> 00:14:36.900
So firstly, a company that has its primary
listing in a particular country may have
very small or even no exposure in their
revenues and earnings to that country.

00:14:36.900 --> 00:14:45.840
And secondly, what's way more important
for us after we've established a country
bias or portfolio bias,

00:14:46.160 --> 00:14:54.900
when we're considering bottom-up
fundamentals, and that's whether it's a
U.S. domestic stock or an international
stock,

00:14:55.320 --> 00:14:58.680
is the actual fundamental performance of
the sector.

00:14:58.680 --> 00:15:09.900
So we take the same approach here with
ADRs and international companies as we do
with all macro indicators.

00:15:10.560 --> 00:15:22.820
At first, we establish a country or
portfolio bias, and then we look at
particular sectors based on the filter of
expected stock performance,

00:15:22.820 --> 00:15:32.000
so volatility, that's based on growth,
x-growth, defensive characteristics, and
also EPS growth profiles.

00:15:33.420 --> 00:15:42.400
And of course, like we always do, we do
further work on positive and negative
outliers and everything in between in the
sector.

00:15:43.080 --> 00:15:52.020
Now, when we're processing and organizing
this data on a sector basis, we can rank
the largest opportunity sets.

00:15:53.180 --> 00:16:00.140
So on a sector basis, the largest amount
of ADRs are characterized as pharma and
biotech,

00:16:00.140 --> 00:16:02.120
so in the pharma and biotech industry.

00:16:02.780 --> 00:16:11.200
And these are mostly untradeable defensive
stocks, and that makes up 15.42% of all
ADRs.

00:16:12.200 --> 00:16:20.520
Next, making up just over 11% of all ADRs
are software and computer services stocks.

00:16:21.120 --> 00:16:27.200
And 35 of those stocks out of 46 are
actually Chinese ADRs.

00:16:28.080 --> 00:16:32.920
Next up is banks, and these make up 6% of
all ADRs.

00:16:33.920 --> 00:16:42.900
And most of these are European and UK
banks, with some developed Asian banks in
Japan and Korea.

00:16:44.040 --> 00:16:49.020
But there's a total of 25 banks at the
time of recording.

00:16:49.820 --> 00:17:00.880
Next sector, after banks, is tech,
hardware, and equipment, which makes up
about 6%, again, of all ADRs.

00:17:01.400 --> 00:17:07.980
And these stocks are dominated by European
companies and Taiwanese companies.

00:17:07.980 --> 00:17:14.960
But there is a total of 24 ADRs in the
tech, hardware, and equipment sector.

00:17:15.660 --> 00:17:22.660
After that comes the financial services
sector, which makes 5.3% of all ADRs.

00:17:22.980 --> 00:17:30.160
And these are mostly Chinese ADRs, which
are very dominant in this particular
sector,

00:17:31.020 --> 00:17:35.600
making up 18 of the 22 ADRs in the sector.

00:17:36.600 --> 00:17:45.540
Next sector, we've got travel and leisure
stocks, travel and leisure ADRs, making up
4.34% of all ADRs.

00:17:46.100 --> 00:17:53.420
And again, this is mostly Chinese ADRs,
covering airlines, hotels, and booking
sites,

00:17:53.620 --> 00:17:56.040
which are actually split fairly evenly.

00:17:57.120 --> 00:18:07.940
After that, we have marine transportation
stocks, making up, again, 4.34% of all
ADRs, totally dominated by Greek stocks.

00:18:08.600 --> 00:18:14.920
So 17 of 18 of those stocks in marine
transportation are Greek shipping stocks.

00:18:16.340 --> 00:18:22.620
Next in the sheet, we actually combined
two sectors, so industrial metals and
mining,

00:18:23.200 --> 00:18:27.640
so hybrid companies, and more purer plays
in mining.

00:18:28.200 --> 00:18:32.280
And this comes out as 4% of all ADRs.

00:18:33.280 --> 00:18:40.340
And this sector is dominated by South
African stocks and Australian stocks.

00:18:40.340 --> 00:18:43.440
But there's a total of 17 of these stocks.

00:18:44.520 --> 00:18:48.580
So you can see how we go about things when
we're doing this.

00:18:49.120 --> 00:18:56.360
We're systematically ranking our
opportunity set for ADRs based on sectors.

00:18:57.420 --> 00:19:02.800
And we can actually go through each of
these sectors and characterize them
further

00:19:02.800 --> 00:19:13.440
and make some interesting observations and
commentary in further detailing the actual
opportunities in each sector.

00:19:14.180 --> 00:19:18.940
So let's start with the pharmaceutical and
biotech sector.

00:19:19.860 --> 00:19:25.840
And from a trader and portfolio manager
perspective, especially for retail,

00:19:26.460 --> 00:19:29.020
this is a very important sector to
discuss.

00:19:29.720 --> 00:19:34.920
Not just in the context of ADRs, but also
in the wider context,

00:19:35.480 --> 00:19:40.300
to include all pharma and biotech stocks
in the United States as well.

00:19:41.200 --> 00:19:47.200
So in our ADR opportunity set,
pharmaceutical and biotech stocks,

00:19:47.840 --> 00:19:54.840
even though it's the largest opportunity
set of any sector in the foreign ADRs
list,

00:19:55.300 --> 00:19:57.740
and it does make up 64 stocks,

00:19:58.020 --> 00:20:04.720
we actually don't recommend that you trade
them at least in your first year as a
retail trader,

00:20:04.960 --> 00:20:06.740
and perhaps even longer.

00:20:07.780 --> 00:20:12.900
And this would also go for US pharma and
biotech stocks as well.

00:20:13.440 --> 00:20:14.540
Now, why is this?

00:20:14.760 --> 00:20:21.680
Well, it's because it's probably the most
difficult sector in the entire stock
market

00:20:21.680 --> 00:20:26.140
to actually have an edge in, even as a
professional trader.

00:20:27.260 --> 00:20:34.780
When you look at the individuals and
organizations or institutional investors
and traders

00:20:34.780 --> 00:20:38.260
that are involved in this sector at the
highest level,

00:20:38.740 --> 00:20:41.400
so if we're looking at hedge funds and
investment banks,

00:20:41.440 --> 00:20:49.540
they have teams of PhD biochemists as both
research analysts and portfolio managers

00:20:49.540 --> 00:20:53.960
that have literally studied chemical
compounds for years.

00:20:54.840 --> 00:21:00.380
And the way pharmaceutical and biotech
stocks work and how they're valued,

00:21:00.940 --> 00:21:04.260
well, they have both current drug
portfolios,

00:21:04.820 --> 00:21:09.620
so drugs that are being sold in the market
right now and making current sales,

00:21:10.180 --> 00:21:11.900
and they have pipeline drugs,

00:21:12.340 --> 00:21:14.640
so drugs that are not currently being
sold,

00:21:15.240 --> 00:21:18.520
but they're going through the approval
process with the FDA.

00:21:18.840 --> 00:21:24.480
So they're going through phase one, phase
two, and phase three approval with the
FDA.

00:21:25.480 --> 00:21:31.000
Now, the fair value or NPV of these
companies' stock prices

00:21:31.000 --> 00:21:35.160
is usually worked out on a per share basis

00:21:35.160 --> 00:21:40.480
by adding the DCF, the discounted cash
flow model of the current portfolio,

00:21:41.060 --> 00:21:47.360
together with a probability-adjusted DCF
of the pipeline portfolio,

00:21:47.720 --> 00:21:51.020
and then we arrive at an NPV per share.

00:21:52.180 --> 00:21:55.680
Now, splitting this up into current and
pipeline,

00:21:55.680 --> 00:22:00.920
the current portfolio will include in
research analyst models

00:22:00.920 --> 00:22:06.320
an assumption of when each drug reaches
peak sales

00:22:06.320 --> 00:22:09.060
and when those drugs come off patent.

00:22:10.380 --> 00:22:15.080
And in the pipeline portfolio, so drugs
that are not yet approved,

00:22:15.780 --> 00:22:18.660
at different phases of the approval
process,

00:22:19.240 --> 00:22:21.640
so phase one, phase two, and phase three,

00:22:22.060 --> 00:22:26.760
at the FDA approval process for the
pipeline drugs,

00:22:27.300 --> 00:22:31.520
what analysts do is they assign
probabilities of approval

00:22:31.520 --> 00:22:36.200
that grow higher at each stage of the
clinical trials.

00:22:37.360 --> 00:22:40.020
So, from a retail trader perspective,

00:22:40.820 --> 00:22:45.660
I mean, there's pretty much no way that
you can have a real edge here

00:22:45.660 --> 00:22:50.220
over many trades in this sector over a
long period of time,

00:22:50.640 --> 00:22:53.880
unless, of course, you're a PhD biochemist

00:22:53.880 --> 00:22:55.960
and an absolute finance whiz,

00:22:56.360 --> 00:22:59.060
or you actually have inside information.

00:23:00.100 --> 00:23:03.800
So, yes, the tradable ADR's opportunity
set

00:23:03.800 --> 00:23:07.800
does increase our overall opportunity set

00:23:07.800 --> 00:23:09.380
in the stock market.

00:23:09.380 --> 00:23:15.720
However, when we get into the details of
the sectors themselves,

00:23:16.060 --> 00:23:20.020
the biggest one, pharmaceutical and
biotech stocks,

00:23:20.200 --> 00:23:24.040
is not very conducive over a long period
of time

00:23:24.040 --> 00:23:26.560
for retail traders to actually have an
edge.

00:23:27.580 --> 00:23:29.960
Certainly, in your first year of trading,

00:23:30.080 --> 00:23:35.340
you should probably be avoiding both
pharmaceutical and biotech stocks

00:23:35.340 --> 00:23:38.940
in ADRs, in the ADR universe,

00:23:39.340 --> 00:23:43.180
and even US domestic listed stocks.

00:23:44.040 --> 00:23:46.260
And I would say, after your first year,

00:23:46.840 --> 00:23:49.720
as long as you're up and making quite a
lot of money,

00:23:50.180 --> 00:23:52.540
at that stage, you will have earned the
right

00:23:52.540 --> 00:23:55.060
to go and take positions

00:23:55.060 --> 00:23:58.620
and in the pharmaceutical and biotech
sectors.

00:23:59.680 --> 00:24:02.860
And as long as you're not betting the farm

00:24:02.860 --> 00:24:08.500
and you're actually implementing sensible
risk management

00:24:08.500 --> 00:24:13.140
with single stock percentage limits on
your portfolio,

00:24:13.640 --> 00:24:17.540
where you don't actually have a large
amount of exposure

00:24:17.540 --> 00:24:19.220
to one idea,

00:24:20.040 --> 00:24:23.660
then yes, as long as you do have an edge

00:24:23.660 --> 00:24:27.020
in a particular farmer or biotech stock,

00:24:27.720 --> 00:24:29.620
then yes, you're essentially,

00:24:30.100 --> 00:24:31.760
because you're making money at this stage

